WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:06.070 align:start position:0%
 
foreign  I'm sure many of you have had an  experience like this where you pitch an  idea or concept and it turns out  someone's already done it in many  respects better a few weeks ago I made a  video where I discussed the  possibilities for an official Zelda  dungeon maker in the same vein as Mario  maker I talked about how I think it  could be done and how to handle some of  the mechanics that come into play when  constructing a dungeon-like environment  My overall conclusions came down to keep  it simple no coding no node setups  nothing too complex that would  discourage the average player after all  just like Mario Maker it's supposed to  be a video game and not a game engine  something everyone can have fun with  turns out there's already a project like  this in development right now Super  Dungeon maker a game where you create  your own dungeons upload them and play  other people's Creations there's even  future plans for a single player story  mode there were quite a few different  projects brought to my attention in the  comments section of my original Zelda  maker video many of them fan projects  like super Zelda maker and z-quest  classic or game engine specifically  designed for 2D action refugees like  Solaris and Legend maker but Super  Dungeon maker was by far the most  popular suggestion and it's easy to see  why the Solaris engine and Legend maker  are both amazing projects however they  are not video games there's no pickup  and play element to them they are PC  only development tools for aspiring game  designers as for super Zelda maker and  z-quest classic again very impressive  projects but in my video I was talking  about something official something that  would actually get published preferably  and selfishly on a platform that I own  and while realistically speaking given  Nintendo's track record there's just no  way they would ever endorse a Zelda fan  project like this let alone publish it  on their own platform regardless of  quality Super Dungeon maker on the other  hand features its own distinct art style  and character so as not to infringe on  any copyright in fact just recently it  widely surpassed its Kickstarter goals  to get the game published on Nintendo  switch so it's well on its way to join  the many Indie gems already present on  the system as such in today's video I  want to talk about the game for a bit to  show its potential compared to the ideas  I talked about in my video and what to  expect from it in the future now full  disclosure this is technically a  sponsorship after seeing my dungeon  maker video the people behind the game  reached out to me to see if I was  perhaps interested in covering their  project since well it does almost  exactly what I talked about in the video  those who are well acquainted with me  and my channel know that I normally  don't do sponsorships I tried it once  and I hated every second of it but I  decided to make an exception because I  did quite literally ask for a game like  this to be made and after spending a few  afternoons playing it I can safely say  that unlike raid Shadow Legends this is  a game I genuinely support and want to  talk about and while yes I am getting  paid a modest fee for my efforts my one  condition was that I would be given  complete freedom to voice my own  opinions and potential criticisms and  not become a Salesman for the game which  they wholeheartedly agreed with with all  that said let's take a look at Super  Dungeon maker first and foremost let me  get this out of the way first because  it's by far the most important aspect of  the game you play as a chicken right  there that's 10 out of 10 game of the  year material because chickens are the  best and if you disagree mifa here wants  to have a strong word with you while  Super Dungeon maker is definitely its  own thing it's also very self-aware  about its Inspirations I mean Our Hero's  name is fink whose ancestors migrated  from a place called henrul like I said  10 out of 10. gameplay wise if you've  played any 2D Zelda you'll feel right at  home five Hearts indicate your health  you attack with a sword there's a hook  shot bombs to blow up walls and blocks  flippers which give you the ability to  swim and a feather to skip across chasms  and Hazards or Dodge attacks in terms of  Graphics I think most will agree that  the game looks absolutely gorgeous the  detailed Sprite work and the fluid  animations it's all amazing it reminds  me how much I wish Nintendo would go  back to the Minish Cap art style someday  hitting enemies just feels so satisfying  I could do this all day  I also like how the sword swings  alternate kind of like a two-head combo  as opposed to the single sword strike in  most 2D zeldas something I never touched  upon in my video is what to do if a  player happens to get stuck somehow  since we're dealing with dungeons made  by everyday people and not professional  level designers accompanied by play  testers and such the chance of  accidentally getting stuck due to an  oversight in a level design is close to  inevitable and did happen to me on more  than one occasion sure you could just  exit the level and start over but this  can be a bit demoralizing if it means  losing a ton of progress especially if  it's a big dungeon the game gets around  this problem by including a sort of  rewind mechanic it'll place you back at  the entrance of the room you're  currently in while also resetting any  progress you've made inside room  including Health pickups chests you've  opened and enemies you've defeated so  it's kind of like a safe State the only  thing I would change about it is that  currently it also restores any damage  you've taken so you can essentially use  it as a bit of a cheat and just keep  resetting the room when you're close to  dying I'd say reset everything except  the HP the mechanic should serve as a  way to avoid soft locks and not as an  easy do-over aside from that it's a  clever fix and it works honestly there's  not a whole lot more to say about the  controls or the gameplay like I said it  plays just like a Zelda game and a  competent one at that I do have a few  complaints like the absence of button  mapping or at the very least the option  to assign Quest items to a face button  of your choice I'm one of those OCD  snobs who always want certain items to  be on a specific face button you know  for muscle memory however it should be  noted that while the game has been  available on Steam since February 2022  as of yet it's still in Early Access and  constantly being worked on and updated  as such I'm also not going to dive into  stuff like performance bugs and glitches  I did encounter my fair share of  anomalies here and there like text boxes  that wouldn't go away or the Hub world  not loading properly but I think it  would be unfair to focus on these types  of issues too much and wait until the  game is you know actually finished I'd  say overall the game runs just fine it  crashed on me once but my capture  software was also acting up that day so  I'm not even sure if the game was to  blame for this that said if you you're  interested in the game yourself and want  to help test and improve the project the  studio has a dedicated Discord server  where you can report your findings and  provide feedback it Bears repeating that  this is a work in progress the same can  be said about the Hub world it's from  here that you'll be able to access to  different game modes like a tutorial  level for Dungeon building the dungeon  Builder itself and the eggs pedia where  you can play other people's creations  and right now that's about all there is  to it there's a few NPCs to talk to give  you tips and funny references but you  cannot enter most of the houses and as  mentioned before there's currently no  story mode it's clear that this is a  part of the game that's still under  construction which is to be expected  with an early access title so let's  instead jump into the main selling point  of the game which is of course the  dungeon editor and here's where it blows  my own ideas completely out of the water  in my video I opted for two separate  editors one for the dungeon map and one  for individual rooms my reasoning was  that since Zelda dungeons tend to have  some sort of transition between rooms it  would make sense to design them  separately and then assemble them in a  map editor so the engine knows where one  will room ends and the other one starts  but Super Dungeon Maker shows that it  can all be done in one editor and it's a  clever system if you create two separate  spaces close to each other the game will  treat them as One Singular room  regardless if they are connected or not  but if you space them out by a certain  amount of tiles I think four the editor  will regard them as separate and draws  an outline around each one this way when  playing the dungeon the engine knows  which areas to load or unload as well as  highlight the room you're currently in  while obscuring the rest this method  also eliminates the problem I mentioned  in my video about doorways potentially  not lining up properly when you create  them in a separate editor here you just  place a doorway in between them and  there you go problem solved as for  shaping designing and decorating the  rooms themselves it works almost exactly  the way I described it in my video so I  don't have much to add here probably the  biggest difference compared to my ideas  aside from the editor itself is the way  puzzles and object interactions are set  up in my examples I propose a system  where you link objects together by  dragging and dropping again similar to  Mario Maker while this does apply to  placing collect Collectibles inside  chests pots grass and so on when it  comes to puzzle elements like switches  spawning items or enemies or triggering  doors to open Super Dungeon makers  approach is quite different for every  self-contained space there's a total of  nine channels available kind of like the  channels on a radio assigning a channel  to an object will cause it to either  Trigger or listen for that channel  depending on what kind of object it is  so for example if you add a floor switch  and a door and set them both to channel  1 the door will open as soon as the  switch is pressed you also have access  to spawners d-spawners and area triggers  which can be used to create puzzles or  traps I'm not going to give a whole  tutorial on how it all works in fact  there's already a dedicated YouTube  channel that explains many of the basics  which I will link in the description but  needless to say you can do a lot of cool  stuff with this system now do I have any  complaints well yeah I do however  similar to the gameplay it's mostly a  matter of wanting to see more  more enemies and traps more puzzle  objects more tiles and dungeon themes  more Quest items and collectibles a  wider selection of music tracks and more  ways to customize the dungeon's look and  feel the amount on offer right now  probably isn't enough to keep most  people invested for very long and  playing other people's Creations for  more than an hour or so did make this  all the more apparent as it didn't take  long before dungeons started to feel  similar same goes for the type of puzzle  setups I was able to concoct with the  options that are available right now  however what is present is a very very  solid basis one with a lot of potential  and hard work behind it the entire game  is being developed by just two people I  mean how crazy is that the devs have  made it clear that they have all the  motivation in the world to keep  expanding the game especially now that  it's slated for a Nintendo switch  release their stretch goals include the  aforementioned story mode more playable  characters besides fink a new dungeon  theme and likely much more depending on  the game's success that's kind of the  beauty of Indie projects like these most  AAA studios are often juggling multiple  IPS and projects at the same time and  kind of reside in an ivory a tower  meanwhile Indie developers are much more  accessible and open to feedback and are  more focused on one project since they  don't have a whole Battalion of people  on the payroll thus the community can  actually be involved in shaping the  final product it's worth mentioning that  the title of this video should obviously  be taken with a grain of salt it's not  meant to be a stab at Nintendo and  there's many valid reasons why they  wouldn't want to publish a Zelda based  dungeon maker of their own be it time  restraints different priorities for the  Zelda IP a lack of inspiration or maybe  they simply don't see it as a worthy  time investment who knows like I said  before a dungeon maker type of game like  this would require continuous expansions  and updates in order to keep a player  base invested over a long period of time  because the player base is the main  driving force behind these type of games  if there's not enough people creating  new dungeons it's basically game over  and as we've seen with the latest Animal  Crossing Nintendo is already having a  hard time staying committed to  long-running projects so maybe a  Community Driven indie project is the  way to go with that said if you see the  same potential I do and want to  contribute you can either back the  project up on Kickstarter by the beta  version on Steam and or join the Discord  Community both of which you can find a  link to in the description below also  despite being Early Access there are  about 1500 dungeons made by the  community available to play right now so  there's plenty enough to keep you busy I  had a lot of fun with the game in the  past week or so and I'm interested to  see what the future holds for it I have  my gripes and wishes but I prefer to  save those for the finished product  possibly the switch version my goal was  not to dissect the game and spend 40  minutes talking about every change I'd  like to see big or small I wanted to  bring it to more people's attention  because I feel like it's a project worth  keeping an eye on and to support it if  you have the means to and that is all  for now a big thanks to Roka play for  reaching out to me and letting me try  out the game to my mods patreons and  channel members and to Morel and sjrd  and Angel drexter for joining you guys  are awesome I will see you all in the  next video this is Dawn signing off and  have a good one  [Music]  thank you  [Music]

